---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Unit from '../components/Unit.astro';
import YinFigure from '../components/figures/YinFigure.astro';
import { unitByMidi } from '../data/site';

const unit = unitByMidi.get(62)!;
---
<Base title="It listens" description={unit.description} note={unit.midi}>
  <Unit unit={unit}>
    <p class="lede" slot="lede" data-split="lines" data-intro data-delay="0.15">
      Piano Tool hears a real piano through the microphone. No cable, no sensor, no plugin.
    </p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every 2,048 samples, about 46 milliseconds of sound at 44.1&nbsp;kHz, the app runs the YIN pitch
        estimator. YIN compares the buffer with delayed copies of itself. When the delay matches one period
        of the note, the difference between the two falls toward zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first dip under 0.1 marks the period. A parabola through its neighbours refines it to a fraction
        of a sample, and the period becomes a frequency, then a MIDI number: 69 plus 12 times the base 2
        logarithm of the frequency over 440.
      </p>
    </div>
    <YinFigure />
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Sample rate</dt><dd>44,100&nbsp;Hz</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Buffer</dt><dd>2,048 samples</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Dip threshold</dt><dd>0.1</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Pitch range</dt><dd>80 to 1,000&nbsp;Hz</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Confidence</dt><dd>0.7 or more</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Quiet buffers are skipped, and so is anything outside 80 to 1,000&nbsp;Hz or with a confidence
        under 0.7. Accuracy is not decided here: the detector only names the pitch, and the scoring engine
        decides whether it was the right note at the right time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Unit>
</Base>
